</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>c. Ammonium sulfate ((NH<sub>4</sub>)<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>132.154 g/mol</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":11,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":["The source asks for relative molecular masses but the guide uses molar-mass units. Relative mass is dimensionless; values coincide numerically."],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"36c8b287a7086a26c49a576e7aaceb1a40e7a12555a36c141f1bab5a19f8640f"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-12","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":12,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":2,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"Calculate the number of moles of: (2) a. 80.2 g Calcium b. 250 g of barium sulfate BaSO4","html":"<div><p>Calculate the number of moles of: (2)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. 80.2 g Calcium</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. 250 g of barium sulfate BaSO<sub>4</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>12. Calculate the number of moles of: (2)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. 80.2 g Calcium</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2.001 mol</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. 250 g of barium sulfate BaSO<sub>4</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>1.071 mol</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":12,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"e1c27e9ef1300874613c947ac25df5ad64d85412f33712c942757909deec66b8"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-13","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":13,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":2,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"Calculate the mass of the following: (2) a. 20.0 mol of lead (II) nitrate Pb(NO3)2 b. 3.5 mol of octane (C8H18)","html":"<div><p>Calculate the mass of the following: (2)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. 20.0 mol of lead (II) nitrate Pb(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. 3.5 mol of octane (C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>18</sub>)</p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>13. Calculate the mass of the following: (2)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. 20.0 mol of lead (II) nitrate Pb(NO<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>6624.4 g</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. 3.5 mol of octane (C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>18</sub>)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>399.8 g</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":13,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"b178900f15566174b100fda6f9c572bf602c5ae2aa2515d13f0eb5df68471c9f"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-14","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":14,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":3,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"What is the concentration, in mol L–1, of a solution in which 40.0 g of sucrose (C12H22O11) is dissolved in 300 mL of the solution? (3)","html":"<div><p>What is the concentration, in mol L<sup>–1</sup>, of a solution\nin which 40.0 g of sucrose</p>\n<p>(C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) is dissolved in 300 mL\nof the solution? (3)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>14. What is the concentration, in mol L<sup>–1</sup>, of a solution\nin which 40.0 g of sucrose</p>\n<p>(C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) is dissolved in 300 mL\nof the solution? (3)</p>\n<p><strong>M(C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) = 342.296 g/mol\n(1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>n(C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) = 40 g/342.296\n= 0.11685 mol (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong><u>c(C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>) = 0.11685\nmol/0.3 L = 0.390 mol/L (1)</u></strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":14,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"468d9269d841f78617b7cc83fa3b7a9095fd1861342176efe6a9306d9945a204"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-15","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":15,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":2,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"What volume of sodium hydroxide solution with a concentration of 0.0606 mol L-1 contains 1.50 g of sodium hydroxide? (2)","html":"<div><p>What volume of sodium hydroxide solution with a concentration\nof</p>\n<p>0.0606 mol L<sup>-1</sup> contains 1.50 g of sodium hydroxide?\n(2)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>15. What volume of sodium hydroxide solution with a concentration\nof</p>\n<p>0.0606 mol L<sup>-1</sup> contains 1.50 g of sodium hydroxide?\n(2)</p>\n<p><strong>n(NaOH) = 1.5/39.998 = 0.0375 mol (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong><u>V = n/c = 0.0375/0.0606 = 0.619 L or 619 mL\n(1)</u></strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":15,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"dd189fe146a08620a9b6fce72632df0e92e892a5fe88d2795a72069ee21c95e0"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-16","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":16,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":2,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"46 g of potassium sulfate is dissolved in 570 mL of water to make a solution. Calculate the concentration of potassium ions (K+) in mol L-1. (2)","html":"<div><p>46 g of potassium sulfate is dissolved in 570 mL of water to make\na solution.</p>\n<p>Calculate the concentration of potassium ions (K<sup>+</sup>) in mol\nL<sup>-1</sup>. (2)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<p><strong>Teacher review required.</strong> The guide assumes final solution volume equals the stated 570 mL of water. Review this approximation.</p><div><p>16. 46 g of potassium sulfate is dissolved in 570 mL of water to make\na solution.</p>\n<p>Calculate the concentration of potassium ions (K<sup>+</sup>) in mol\nL<sup>-1</sup>. (2)</p>\n<p><strong>n(K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) = 46/174.27 = 0.264\nmol</strong></p>\n<p><strong>c(K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) = 0.264/0.57 = 0.463 mol/L\n(1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong><u>c(K<sup>+</sup>) = 2 x c(K<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) =\n0.463 x 2 = 0.926 mol/L (1)</u></strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":16,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":["The guide assumes final solution volume equals the stated 570 mL of water. Review this approximation."],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"969cca427b25b5eb2687d0c6150161156d5873fa5fae79be7c9aec96a780a03f"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-17","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":17,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":2,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"A saturated solution of calcium carbonate (CaCO3) was found to contain 0.0198 g of calcium carbonate in 2000 g of solution. Calculate the concentration of calcium carbonate in this solution. (2)","html":"<div><p>A saturated solution of calcium carbonate (CaCO<sub>3</sub>) was\nfound to contain</p>\n<p>0.0198 g of calcium carbonate in 2000 g of solution. Calculate\nthe</p>\n<p>concentration of calcium carbonate in this solution. (2)</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<p><strong>Teacher review required.</strong> The question paper omits the requested concentration unit; the matching guide explicitly asks for ppm (9.9 ppm by mass).</p><div><p>17. A saturated solution of calcium carbonate (CaCO<sub>3</sub>) was\nfound to contain</p>\n<p>0.0198 g of calcium carbonate in 2000 g of solution. Calculate\nthe</p>\n<p>concentration of calcium carbonate in this solution in ppm. (2)</p>\n<p><strong>Conversions correct: (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Correct result (1)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>c(CaCO<sub>3</sub>) = 19.8 mg/2kg = 9.9 ppm</strong></p>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":17,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":["The question paper omits the requested concentration unit; the matching guide explicitly asks for ppm (9.9 ppm by mass)."],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"4ba28a9386c92e8380603bd796ae4c43e437dac0e47896c1a13fba381717380b"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-18","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":18,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":5,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"Balance the following chemical equations. (5) a. CaCO3(s) + HCl(aq) → CaCl2(aq) + CO2(g) + H2O(l) b. Na2SO4(aq) + Pb(NO3)2 (aq) → PbSO4(s) + NaNO3(aq) c. Mg(NO3)2(aq) + Na3PO4(aq) → Mg3(PO4)2(s) + NaNO3(aq) d. N2 (g) + H2 (g) → NH3 (g) e. Mg (s) + HCl (aq) → MgCl2 (aq) + H2 (g)","html":"<div><p>Balance the following chemical equations. (5)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. CaCO3<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + HCl<sub>(aq)</sub> →\nCaCl2<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> + CO2<em><sub>(g)</sub></em> +\nH2O<em><sub>(l)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. Na2SO4<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> + Pb(NO3)2 <em><sub>(aq)</sub></em>\n→ PbSO4<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + NaNO3<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>c. Mg(NO3)2<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> + Na3PO4<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em>\n→ Mg3(PO4)2<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + NaNO3<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>d. N<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(g)</sub></em> + H<sub>2</sub>\n<em><sub>(g)</sub></em> → NH<sub>3 <em>(g)</em></sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>e. Mg <em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + HCl <em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> →\nMgCl<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> + H<sub>2</sub>\n<em><sub>(g)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>18. Balance the following chemical equations. (5)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>a. CaCO3<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + <strong>2</strong>\nHCl<sub>(aq)</sub> → CaCl2<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> +\nCO2<em><sub>(g)</sub></em> + H2O<em><sub>(l)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>b. Na2SO4<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> + Pb(NO3)2 <em><sub>(aq)</sub></em>\n→ PbSO4<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + <strong>2</strong>\nNaNO3<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>c. <strong>3</strong> Mg(NO3)2<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> +\n<strong>2</strong> Na3PO4<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> →\nMg3(PO4)2<em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + <strong>6</strong>\nNaNO3<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>d. N<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(g)</sub></em> + <strong>3</strong>\nH<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(g)</sub></em> → <strong>2</strong> NH<sub>3\n<em>(g)</em></sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>e. Mg <em><sub>(s)</sub></em> + <strong>2</strong> HCl\n<em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> → MgCl<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(aq)</sub></em> +\nH<sub>2</sub> <em><sub>(g)</sub></em></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":18,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"bf938465b57b9f89030be07057ec20e6493f0d6349942c5b035e6837c02e136b"},{"id":"chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016-written-19","subject":"Chemistry","year":12,"examYear":2016,"units":"3 & 4","number":19,"source":"Chemical Fundamentals — Test 1 (All Saints, 2016)","section":"Short answer","marks":12,"topics":["Chemical fundamentals"],"subtopics":["Chemical Fundamentals"],"mixed":false,"text":"Write balanced ionic equations for the following reactions.(Note: a correctly balanced molecular equation including state symbols will attract two marks, a third mark is given for an ionic equation.) (12) a. Reacting solid aluminium with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce aqueous aluminium chloride and hydrogen gas. b. Adding a solution of silver nitrate to a solution of sodium chloride to form a precipitate of silver chloride and aqueous sodium nitrate. c. Butane (C4H10) burns in an excess of oxygen to form carbon dioxide and water. d. When sodium is added to water, the products are a solution of sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and hydrogen gas","html":"<div><p>Write balanced ionic equations for the following reactions.(Note:\na correctly balanced molecular equation including state symbols will\nattract two marks, a third mark is given for an ionic equation.)</p>\n<p>(12)</p>\n<p>a. Reacting solid aluminium with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce\naqueous aluminium chloride and hydrogen gas.</p>\n<p>b. Adding a solution of silver nitrate to a solution of sodium\nchloride to form a precipitate of silver chloride and aqueous sodium\nnitrate.</p>\n<p>c. Butane (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>) burns in an excess of oxygen\nto form carbon dioxide and water.</p>\n<p>d. When sodium is added to water, the products are a solution of\nsodium hydroxide (NaOH) and hydrogen gas</p>\n</div>","answerHtml":"<div><p>19. Write balanced ionic equations for the following reactions.(Note:\na correctly balanced molecular equation including state symbols will\nattract two marks, a third mark is given for an ionic equation.)</p>\n<p>(12)</p>\n<p>a. Reacting solid aluminium with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce\naqueous aluminium chloride and hydrogen gas.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2 Al <sub>(s)</sub> + 6 HCl <sub>(aq)</sub> --&gt; 2\nAlCl<sub>3 (aq)</sub> + 3 H<sub>2</sub> <sub>(g)</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2 Al + 6 H<sup>+</sup> --&gt; 2 Al <sup>3+</sup> + 3\nH<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>b. Adding a solution of silver nitrate to a solution of sodium\nchloride to form a precipitate of silver chloride and aqueous sodium\nnitrate.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>AgNO<sub>3</sub> <sub>(aq)</sub> + NaCl (<sub>aq)</sub>\n--&gt; AgCl <sub>(s)</sub> + NaNO<sub>3 (aq)</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ag<sup>+</sup> + Cl<sup>-</sup> --&gt; AgCl</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>c. Butane (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>) burns in an excess of oxygen\nto form carbon dioxide and water.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2 C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub> <sub>(g)</sub> + 13 O<sub>2\n(g)</sub> --&gt; 10 H<sub>2</sub>O <sub>(l)</sub> + 8 CO<sub>2\n(g)</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>d. When sodium is added to water, the products are a solution of\nsodium hydroxide (NaOH) and hydrogen gas</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2 Na <sub>(s)</sub> + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O <sub>(l)</sub> --&gt;\n2 NaOH <sub>(aq)</sub> + H<sub>2 (g)</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>2Na + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O --&gt; 2 Na<sup>+</sup> + 2\nOH<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub></strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n</div>","options":[],"optionsHtml":[],"correctAnswer":null,"markingMode":"manual","solutionOrigin":"supplied","sourceFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourcePath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.doc","sourceBlobSHA":"4767055f1c5bc24b21f897fd977c8c5144e22bca","sourceSHA256":"8b12c6765c3f6587bef331006d3110a6b04adc85e197c14a70345f3129b4eea8","sourceKeyFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyPath":"resources/ATAR/Chemistry/Assessments/12/All Saints/2016/2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals Answers.doc","sourceKeyBlobSHA":"750a119dac9c8ca589776e8ef67abc2f22fc5f7e","sourceKeySHA256":"6787d6daae297bef406c809e7cbea72b98b1ed0b06352be08fc07f8ffbbe60dd","sourceKeyNumber":19,"bankFile":"practice-studio-chemistry-sixth-fundamentals2016.json","addedAt":"2026-11-07T05:47:40.476409Z","editableText":true,"questionFormat":"native-text","fontFamily":"Arial","fontSizePt":12,"responseLines":0,"sourceReviewNotes":[],"sourceTextFile":"2016 Test 1 Chem Fundamentals.docx","types":["Short answer"],"figures":0,"hash":"3715664d36770c97bccd777f165b3c93fa7350e2657a0691a523cd0191c7763a"}]